rows=[(429, 'a-wall-becomes-sky', 'A wall becomes sky', 'The speaker once shared a view with the sky; a neighboring wall now blocks it, and imagination turns that wall into sky. Preserve the ordinary obstruction and leave imagined figures in the words.', 'Original horizontal 3:2 quiet gouache and charcoal interior, viewed from a low resting height beside a simple bed. Only a small corner of plain cotton bedcover enters the lower left. Ahead is a modest rectangular open window or unglazed wall opening; outside it a nearby tall plaster wall fills almost the entire view, with a very narrow pale blue sliver of sky above its top edge. Believable thick window reveal and adjacent wall distance; soft side daylight grazes a few ordinary plaster discolorations, nothing shaped like a face or constellation. Large quiet wall surface, no picturesque decay, barred prison, decorative curtains, stars, birds, airplanes, people, text or fantasy forms. The restrained contrast is between blocked sight and mental openness, not an attractive travel window.'), (434, 'between-short-flights', 'Between short flights', 'The poem follows a young bird from tentative flights to independent nesting. An unheroic pause on a branch carries the mixture of fatigue, freedom and parental apprehension without showing a triumphant symbolic eagle.', 'Original horizontal 3:2 loose graphite and soft moss-green watercolor bird study. One small ordinary brown fledgling rests on a slender branch left of center, seen three-quarter from behind; wings folded correctly, a short tail, a slightly soft ruffled back and two little feet gripping the same branch. Its head turns gently toward an open pale patch at right, beak in small side profile, no oversized human-like eye. A few overlapping leaves obscure part of the branch, enough continuity to show it supports the bird. Most of the picture remains pale quiet air, branch fades in and out through foliage. No eagle, nest full of babies, second bird, planets, heroic wingspread, ornate plumage or perfectly outlined feathers. Alert but tired after a short flight; ordinary overcast daylight, no spotlight.'), (435, 'the-line-now-quiet', 'The line now quiet', 'The poem moves from telephone conversations to a bond sustained beyond technology. A quiet receiver and relaxed cord suggest what no longer has to be said, without inventing a couple or literal birds nesting on cables.', 'Original horizontal 3:2 spare matte oil-pastel drawing with muted umber and warm-grey gouache on opaque ivory paper. A simple dark old telephone receiver rests on an ordinary table near the left edge, lying on its side at a slight diagonal, both mouthpiece and earpiece connected by one gently curved handle. A single coiled cord exits from the receiver and forms a relaxed partial curve toward the cropped lower edge. Coils compress naturally near the receiver and open slightly farther away; no tangled infinity loop. The telephone base stays outside the frame. Soft local contact shadows anchor the receiver and cord; broad unused tabletop at right. No numbers, dial, labels, text, lit screen, flowers, people or hands. A modest object after a long conversation, calm warmth rather than lonely abandoned decay.')]
items=[]
for pid,ident,title,reason,scene in rows:
 p=S/f'content/editions/issue-21/poem-{pid}.json';d=json.loads(p.read_text());snapshot=B/f'poem-{pid}.json';snapshot.write_bytes(p.read_bytes())
